<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Andrés de Urdaneta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1508">1508</a>–<a href="/years/1568">1568</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Andrés de Urdaneta was a Spanish navigator and Augustinian friar. Sailing with the expedition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-lopez-de-legazpi">Miguel López de Legazpi</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> in 1565, he found the return route across the Pacific to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> by sailing north to catch the westerly winds, a route known as the tornaviaje. It made possible the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila-galleon">Manila galleon</a> trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> for 250 years.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andres-de-urdaneta.jpg" alt="Andrés de Urdaneta" width="213" height="332" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andres_de_Urdaneta.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Andrés de Urdaneta was born in 1508 near Ordizia, a Basque town then called Villafranca, in the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. His father, Juan Ochoa de Urdaneta, served as its mayor. At seventeen he joined the Loaísa expedition to the Spice Islands as a page to Juan Sebastián Elcano. Seven ships left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/a-coruna">A Coruña</a> on 24 July 1525; storms and desertions reduced the fleet, and both Loaísa and Elcano died. The last ship reached the Spice Islands in October 1526 with 105 of the original 450 men.</p><p>Urdaneta stayed there for more than eight years, acting as an emissary to local leaders, scouting and leading sorties against the Portuguese. A powder explosion burned and disfigured him. He left on 15 February 1535 and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> on 26 June 1536, where the Portuguese confiscated his maps and journals. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valladolid">Valladolid</a> he wrote a report for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-the-indies">Council of the Indies</a> and received 60 gold ducats.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He sailed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> on 16 October 1538 as chief navigator for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-de-alvarado">Pedro de Alvarado</a>, who was killed in a native rebellion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-galicia">New Galicia</a> in 1540. Urdaneta then worked for Viceroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-de-mendoza">Antonio de Mendoza</a> and in 1543 became corregidor of a district in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michoacan">Michoacán</a> and visitador. In 1552 he joined the Augustinians, professing his vows in 1553 and being ordained a priest in 1557.</p><p>In 1559 Philip II ordered the viceroy to secure his services for an expedition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. Urdaneta declined to lead it and recommended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-lopez-de-legazpi">Miguel López de Legazpi</a>, serving as adviser and head of the Augustinian missionaries. The fleet left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-navidad">La Navidad</a> on 21 November 1564, reached Guam on 23 January 1565 and Samar on 13 February, and settled on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cebu">Cebu</a>. Urdaneta founded the first churches in the Philippines.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The San Pedro, commanded by Legazpi's grandson Felipe de Salcedo, left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cebu">Cebu</a> on 1 June 1565 with Urdaneta advising. It sailed northeast to the 38th parallel, where westerly winds carried it across the Pacific, sighted Catalina Island on 18 September and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acapulco">Acapulco</a> on 8 October after four months and eight days. Only 18 men could still sail the ship. Another ship under Alonso de Arellano had reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> first, but he was suspected of mutiny and desertion and his account was vague, so Urdaneta was recognised as the pioneer of the route. Spanish ships, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila-galleon">Manila galleon</a>, used it for more than 200 years. He died at the friary in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> on 3 June 1568.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Andrés de Urdaneta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ordizia, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.06,-2.18&amp;year=1547" aria-label="Ordizia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Andrés de Urdaneta"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4864 3892h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4864 3892h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.06,-2.18&amp;year=1547">What was here in 1547? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
